      "id": 1,
      "text": "Collinwood was founded in 1913 as a stop on the Tennessee Western Railroad, built to serve the local iron industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collinwood%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name combines “Collin” for its founder and “wood” for the abundant timber industry that once thrived there.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofcollinwood.org/index.php/collinwood-history",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1920s a silent‑movie theater drew crowds to see stars like Hoot Gibson, making Collinwood a regional entertainment hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofcollinwood.org/index.php/collinwood-history",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Collinwood sits on a broad plain surrounded by low hills, with eastern streams feeding Shoal Creek and western ones draining into Indian Creek.",
